From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: vfe-17x: Keep the buffers of a frame on one frame number

The pixel path numbers its image and statistics buffers by the epochs
seen since the stream start: a buffer done after epoch k is frame k.
Several cases broke that, seen as statistics, parameters or RAW one
frame away from the images:

- A buffer done before the first epoch belongs to a frame that began
  before the stream, yet was numbered frame 0, like the first real frame
  after it. Write the buffer again instead, by the first frame: the
  statistics write master gets its address again, an image or RDI
  buffer's address is queued again after the one already queued, and the
  buffer bookkeeping follows that order.
- The interrupt handler counted the epoch before the buffers done in the
  same interrupt, which belong to the frame before. Handle the epoch
  last.
- The RDI lines numbered their frames on their own. While the pixel path
  runs, number the RDI frames of the same VFE by its epochs too, so that
  the RAW buffer and the processed buffer of one sensor frame carry the
  same sequence.
- The parameters, statistics, image and RDI buffers of a request decided
  their frame at different points: an image or RDI address went to the
  write master when queued, the parameters at the next epoch, a
  statistics buffer at the end of a frame. When the image path ran out
  of buffers and skipped frames, a buffer queued into its empty address
  queue was written into the current frame while its parameters took the
  next one, and every later request stayed a frame apart.

While the pixel path runs, settle frame k + 1 at epoch k: the image path
gets its next buffer then, the RDI lines get theirs only for a frame the
image path writes, and frame k + 1 takes the next parameters and a
statistics buffer only if the image path writes it; otherwise it writes
its grid to the scratch buffer and the parameters wait. A buffer queued
after epoch k still joins frame k + 1 when it lacks one, with its
parameters applied then. Done buffers no longer pull the next address,
and a line being disabled takes no more: vfe_disable_output() marks it
stopping under the output lock.

Tested with consumers 25-60 ms slow, two buffers per node at the full
frame rate, starvation and recovery after a bus error: the image,
parameters, statistics and RAW of every request stay on one frame.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
